Guard: acceptMatchers / ClassNameMatcher allowlist (default-deny) via ObjectSerializationCodecFactory.accept(Pattern).
Mechanism: deserialization-allowlist ; class_kind: benign-shadow-class (PcbSeed, readObject channel).

RUN MODE = real-server-tcp (Apache MINA is a NETWORK framework; its real entry point is a TCP server):
  * Each version runs a REAL MINA NioSocketAcceptor TCP server (app/Server.java) on port 9009 with
    ProtocolCodecFilter(new ObjectSerializationCodecFactory()) and acceptMatchers configured (via
    reflection, so one source compiles on V0=2.2.3) to accept ONLY the legit class PcbExpected -- NOT PcbSeed.
  * A SEPARATE `client` container (app/Client.java) connects over TCP across the docker network and
    writes a length-prefixed MINA serialized stream (4-byte BE length == MINA getInt(), then the body).
    This is a GENUINE EXTERNAL network trigger (real TCP, separate process/container), the way MINA is
    actually used. No MINA code is edited; nothing is deserialized in-process by a harness.
  * PcbSeed.readObject() reads the planted /canary/flag.txt into a transient `flag` field ONLY during
    server-side reconstruction; the server IoHandler echoes it back over the SAME TCP connection.

Runtime: maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-11. Maven Central pulls the EXACT org.apache.mina:mina-core per version.

